WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.507
Joins need extra attention in a distributed system.

00:00:03.507 --> 00:00:12.127
First, the same relational rule still applies: confirm the join keys and understand the relationship between the tables.

00:00:12.127 --> 00:00:17.927
A one-to-many join can multiply rows whether you are using Postgres or Spark.

00:00:17.927 --> 00:00:23.737
Second, Spark may need to move data between workers so matching keys can meet.

00:00:23.737 --> 00:00:26.427
That movement can be expensive.

00:00:26.427 --> 00:00:30.107
Before a large join, ask three questions.

00:00:30.107 --> 00:00:34.152
Are the keys correct? Is the output grain controlled?

00:00:34.152 --> 00:00:37.624
And can either side be reduced safely before the join?
